\subsection{Stress-Model Equations}
Uniaxial compressive strength is evaluated using:
\[
\sigma_c = \frac{F_{\text{max}}}{A},
\]
where $F_{\text{max}}$ is peak load and $A$ is specimen cross-sectional
area.

Thermal shock resistance is assessed via:
\[
\Delta T = T_{\text{hot}} - T_{\text{cold}},
\qquad
\epsilon_{\text{thermal}} = \alpha \Delta T,
\]
with $\alpha$ the thermal expansion coefficient.

Dynamic vibration damping is modeled using:
\[
\zeta = \frac{c}{2\sqrt{km}},
\]
where $c$ is damping coefficient, $k$ stiffness, and $m$ mass.

Hydrostatic permeability is measured using Darcy's law:
\[
k = \frac{Q \mu L}{A \Delta P},
\]
with $k$ permeability, $Q$ volumetric flow, $\mu$ fluid viscosity,
$L$ specimen thickness, and $\Delta P$ pressure differential.

